> Dear Michigan Society of Fellows Postdoctoral Fellowship Committee,
> 
> I am pleased to enthusiastically recommend Mohammad Taky Tahmid for your Postdoctoral Fellowship. Tahmid is in the final semester of his PhD in Environmental Economics at the University of Delaware, where I act as his primary advisor, and he has been a steady and important contributor to several research efforts in our group. His work spans agriculture-labor dynamics, empirical climate–economy analysis, analysis of environmental pollution on health, and several applied projects involving climate, fishing, and environmental data. He is one of the most broadly capable students I have worked with in recent years.
> 
> Tahmid’s proposed project, “Beyond the Cash Wage,” is a strong and timely extension of his broader research agenda on climate, agriculture, and welfare, which has focused on Bangladesh. The determinants of agricultural labor wages are of first-order importance in many lower-income countries, and Tahmid’s research recognizes the complex interactions between workers, farm owners, and field outcomes. He has already brought together multiple kinds of wage, yield, and environmental data, but it became clear during his PhD that a complete picture of these interactions would require a further series of projects. In particular, the proposal brings in a structured interview and survey process, to better understand the patterns he sees in the data. This information is relevant both to climate adaptation investment in Bangladesh and better understanding the risk of climate change and heat waves across the developing world. Labor productivity is one of the most economically significant impacts of climate change, but real-world evidence on it remains limited. In addition to the academic contribution, Tahmid’s work has the potential to feed directly into national-scale climate adaptation planning and labor interventions.
> 
> His prior work makes him particularly well prepared to carry out this research. Tahmid has taken on substantial responsibilities in three major funded projects. In the DNREC Information Gaps Analysis, he helped assemble and organize a large set of marine datasets used to support the state’s marine spatial planning work. This included cleaning, cataloging, and integrating nearly 300 datasets, which required close attention to detail and an understanding of how different physical and socioeconomic layers fit together. He created a geodatabase from this work in support of DNREC’s Ocean and Bay Plan. This work forms the basis of the paper “Data Gaps off the Delaware Coast: A Spatial Approach to Data Gaps for Marine Spatial Planning,” where he is the first author. 
> 
> He is also a co-author on our ongoing paper on current economic losses from climate change. Tahmid led the development of the international trade spillover component, using input–output tables. This was a complicated part of the analysis, for which he self-organized his time studying a new area and performing analysis leading to an exciting new solution to the estimation of climate spill-overs. This paper is currently under revision at Nature.
> 
> 
> 
> In support of the Climate and Clean Air Coalition’s (CCAC) Economic Assessment, Tahmid has been working on the PAGE integrated assessment model. He helped develop new models of future 
> 
> PM₂.₅ concentrations and implemented these into the PAGE model. He also helped me to integrate mortality risks from heat into this model. This work has required learning new tools and codebases, and he has done so effectively. This innovative assessment has been selected as a spotlight report by UNEP.
> 
> Tahmid’s dissertation reflects the same range of skills.
> •	His first chapter examines the role of meals provided to farmer workers in Bangladesh, to study the risks these workers are exposed to and the drivers behind their wages.
> •	His second chapter combines empirical models of the global crop losses from climate change with simulations from three biophysical process agriculture models, to identify gaps and find ways to improve future projections of these losses.
> •	His third chapter is based on a large-scale recreational fishing survey that he co-designed and administered, and its implications for natural resource governance across the Mid-Atlantic.
> 
> Across these chapters, he has assembled large datasets, adapted methods from the literature, and developed his own econometric specifications after working through several alternatives. His work is excellent and contributes to ongoing discussions about climate impacts on agriculture and welfare.
> 
> Before joining UD, Tahmid taught at the Dhaka School of Economics and other institutions. Although he has not served as a TA here (a failing of our program), he is a great resource to his classmates. He often assists others with coding questions, talks through research ideas with them, and helps with data handling or general project setup. He has also been a reliable source of support for other members of our research group when they need help getting started on coding or analysis tasks.
> 
> Tahmid has also been active in student organizations on campus. He currently serves as Treasurer of the Muslim Student Association and represented the School of Marine Science and Policy (SMSP) in the Graduate Student Government. He previously served on the SMSP Director’s Council as the student representative from the Newark campus and has participated in mentorship programs like i-Buddy for new international students. These activities have helped build community and support students across the UD campus.
> 
> Overall, Tahmid has shown consistent excellence and has taken on work that has contributed significantly to both our research group and broader knowledge. He works well independently, communicates clearly, and is reliable in completing tasks. I believe he would be an ideal candidate for this postdoc, and I am strongly recommending him for it.
