> I would not extend it merely for length: just over two pages is already substantial. The strongest addition would be **evaluation rather than more inventory**—helping the committee understand how Tahmid compares with peers and why he will thrive in an interdisciplinary Fellows community.
> 
> Consider adding a short paragraph on:
> 
> - **Intellectual independence and trajectory.** Explain a moment when he identified a question, method, or solution independently—not just executed a project. State plainly that he is becoming an independent scholar with a distinctive Bangladesh-centered climate–labor–development agenda.
> - **Fit for the Michigan Society of Fellows.** Emphasize his ability to communicate across economics, environmental science, public health, data science, and policy—and his likely contribution to a residential interdisciplinary community. This is particularly important for a fellowship rather than a standard postdoc.
> - **Comparative assessment.** A calibrated sentence is valuable: e.g., “Among the PhD students I have advised/worked with over [N] years, he ranks among the strongest in [technical breadth, persistence, interdisciplinary judgment].” Only use a comparison you can defend.
> - **Teaching and mentoring potential.** You already note informal peer support, but one concrete example of how he explains methods, supports newer students, or draws on his prior teaching would make this more persuasive.
> - **Character under uncertainty.** A brief example of persistence, intellectual generosity, sound judgment, or responsiveness to feedback can distinguish him from an accomplished CV.
